                <pb facs="0970" n="962" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg962"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.00/912: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-24T19:00:00+09:00">June 24, 1940—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 24—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>489. Our 486, June 23, 6 p.m.<anchor n="20" xml:id="d1077-f1"/> Today Prince
                    Konoye resigned as President of the Privy Council. There are rumors to the
                    effect that the resignation is preliminary to the formation of a new Konoye
                    Cabinet, and that alternatively or concurrently he will assume leadership of the
                    new Single Party Movement.</p>
                <pb facs="0971" n="963" xml:id="pg963"/>
                <p>Mr. Yoshimichi Hara, formerly Vice-President of the Privy Council, replaces
                    Prince Konoye as President. His appointment is without special significance.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
